    text: "FICTIONAL DEMONSTRATION DOCUMENT. Velmara (velmaratinib) and NX-441 are invented products. No real patients, studies, organisations or people are described.\n\nVELMARA (velmaratinib) — FIELD MEDICAL INSIGHTS REPORT\nQ3 2026 (July to September)\nPrepared by: Medical Science Liaison (MSL) team, US and EU5\nSource: 184 documented scientific exchanges with healthcare professionals (HCPs)\nStatus: Internal. Insights are aggregated and anonymised; no individual HCP is identified.\n\n1. OVERVIEW\n\nInteractions by setting: 58 percent academic centres, 42 percent community practices.\nInteractions by role: 71 percent medical oncologists, 9 percent neuro-oncologists, 8 percent radiation oncologists, 7 percent oncology nurses and nurse practitioners, 5 percent pharmacists.\nInteractions by region: 52 percent US, 48 percent EU5.\n\nThe five most frequent insight themes this quarter are below, with the share of interactions in which each theme came up.\n\n2. THEME 1 — BRAIN METASTASES AND INTRACRANIAL DURABILITY (raised in 46 percent of interactions)\n\nHCPs consistently asked about intracranial efficacy after osimertinib progression. The most common follow-up question was about durability: \"How long does the intracranial response last?\"\n\nRepresentative insights:\n- \"Response rate in the brain is not enough; I need to know it holds.\" (academic medical oncologist, US)\n- \"Half my patients at progression have new or growing brain lesions. If Velmara delays radiotherapy, that matters a lot.\" (radiation oncologist, EU5)\n\nInsight: there is limited evidence on CNS response and duration of response in brain metastases after osimertinib. HCPs know the VEL-301 CNS sub-study is small.\n\n3. THEME 2 — SEQUENCING AFTER OSIMERTINIB (raised in 38 percent)\n\nHCPs are unsure how to sequence Velmara, NX-441 and chemotherapy. Community oncologists in particular asked for real-world experience rather than trial data.\n\nRepresentative insights:\n- \"I need to see what my peers are doing after osimertinib and how patients do.\" (community medical oncologist, US)\n- \"Is there a reason to use NX-441 before Velmara, or the other way round?\" (academic medical oncologist, EU5)\n\nInsight: real-world treatment sequencing after osimertinib failure, including Velmara versus NX-441, is an unanswered question.\n\n4. THEME 3 — OLDER AND FRAIL PATIENTS (raised in 33 percent)\n\nRepresentative insights:\n- \"The trial patients are younger and fitter than mine. What happens in my 78-year-old with heart disease?\" (community medical oncologist, US)\n- \"Families carry a lot of the burden with oral drugs: monitoring, transport, side effects at home. Nobody measures that.\" (oncology nurse, EU5)\n- \"Show me quality of life in patients like the ones I treat, not only EORTC in a trial.\" (academic medical oncologist, EU5)\n\nInsights:\n- Comparative effectiveness in elderly patients versus the standard of care they would otherwise receive is not established.\n- There is insufficient evidence on caregiver burden and on PROs in frail patients.\n\n5. THEME 4 — SAFETY MONITORING IN ROUTINE CARE (raised in 27 percent)\n\nRepresentative insights:\n- \"We do not do routine ECGs in our satellite clinics. I need a practical monitoring plan.\" (community medical oncologist, US)\n- \"How quickly does ILD come on, and what should a community practice do about it?\" (pharmacist, US)\n\nInsight: HCPs want characterisation of ILD and QT incidence in routine care, including monitoring outside academic centres.\n\n6. THEME 5 — ACCESS AND COST (raised in 19 percent, mostly US)\n\nRepresentative insights:\n- \"Our pathway committee will want persistence data before they put it ahead of NX-441.\" (community practice pharmacist, US)\n- \"Payers are already asking about IRA pricing for the class.\" (US IDN oncology lead)\n- \"Recurrence is expensive: repeat scans, biopsies, admissions. Is anyone measuring that?\" (academic medical oncologist, US)\n\nInsights:\n- Six-month discontinuation and persistence in US routine care are expected by pathway committees.\n- HCPs and IDNs perceive a high but unquantified burden of recurrence (emergency department use, hospitalisations, outpatient visits).\n\n7. LOWER-FREQUENCY THEMES\n\n- Long-term overall survival (raised in 12 percent): HCPs accept that OS will be immature at first read-out but want a plan for longer follow-up.\n- Congress presence (raised in 4 percent, mostly by internal stakeholders): requests for more symposia. Field medical regards this as a communication topic, not an evidence gap.\n\n8. RECOMMENDATIONS FROM FIELD MEDICAL\n\n1. Prioritise evidence on intracranial durability after osimertinib.\n2. Generate real-world sequencing evidence after osimertinib, including Velmara versus NX-441.\n3. Address the elderly comparative question and PROs and caregiver burden in frail patients.\n4. Provide practical ILD and QT monitoring guidance and real-world safety data from community settings.\n5. Support HEOR with insights on persistence and on the burden of recurrence.\n\nEND OF DOCUMENT",
